The double sum is over all ordered pairs \((j, k)\) of positive integers. We can reindex the sum by letting \(n = j + k\). For a fixed sum \(n \ge 2\), the positive integer solutions \((j, k)\) are \(j = 1, 2, \dots, n-1\) with \(k = n - j\). Thus there are exactly \(n-1\) pairs for each \(n\). The sum becomes

\[
\sum_{j=1}^\infty \sum_{k=1}^\infty \frac{1}{(j+k)^3}
= \sum_{n=2}^\infty \frac{n-1}{n^3}
= \sum_{n=2}^\infty \left( \frac{1}{n^2} - \frac{1}{n^3} \right).
\]

